<h2>Seizures — อาการชัก</h2>
<blockquote>"ป้องกันการบาดเจ็บ ไม่ใช่หยุดชัก — ชักจะหยุดเอง"</blockquote>
<h3>ภาพรวบ</h3>
<p>Seizure = abnormal electrical discharge ในสมอง → involuntary muscle activity</p>
<p>ใน EMS: <strong>Protect the patient, don't stop the seizure</strong></p>
<h3>Types of Seizures</h3>
<h4>Generalized Seizures</h4>
<table>
<tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Tonic-Clonic (Grand Mal)</strong></td><td>Tonic phase (stiffening 10-20 sec) → Clonic phase (jerking 30-60 sec) → Post-ictal (confusion, fatigue)</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Absence (Petit Mal)</strong></td><td>Brief staring spell, 5-10 sec, no post-ictal (common in children)</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Tonic</strong></td><td>Stiffening only</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Atonic</strong></td><td>Loss of muscle tone → sudden fall ("drop attack")</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Myoclonic</strong></td><td>Brief jerks</td></tr>
</table>
<h4>Focal (Partial) Seizures</h4>
<table>
<tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Focal aware</strong></td><td>Patient remains aware, may have motor/sensory symptoms</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Focal impaired awareness</strong></td><td>Altered consciousness, automatisms (lip smacking, fumbling)</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Focal → bilateral tonic-clonic</strong></td><td>Starts focal, spreads to generalized</td></tr>
</table>
<h4>Status Epilepticus ⚠️</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Seizure ≥5 minutes</strong> OR <strong>≥2 seizures without regaining consciousness</strong></li><li><strong>Medical emergency</strong> — brain damage risk increases with duration</li><li><strong>Mortality 10-20%</strong> if prolonged</li></ul>
<h3>Causes of Seizures (VITAMINS)</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Letter</th><th>Cause</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>V</strong></td><td>Vascular (stroke, hemorrhage, AVM)</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>I</strong></td><td>Infection (meningitis, encephalitis, brain abscess)</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>T</strong></td><td>Trauma (head injury, post-traumatic epilepsy)</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>A</strong></td><td>Alcohol withdrawal, drug withdrawal</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>M</strong></td><td>Metabolic (hypoglycemia, hyponatremia, hypocalcemia, uremia)</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>I</strong></td><td>Intoxication (cocaine, amphetamines, TCA, theophylline)</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>N</strong></td><td>Neoplasm (brain tumor, metastasis)</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>S</strong></td><td>Seizure disorder (epilepsy), non-compliance with meds</td></tr>
</table>
<h4>Common Causes in EMS</h4>
<p>1. <strong>Epilepsy + non-compliance</strong> (most common)</p>
<p>2. <strong>Hypoglycemia</strong> — always check glucose!</p>
<p>3. <strong>Alcohol withdrawal</strong> (6-48 hours after last drink)</p>
<p>4. <strong>Head trauma</strong></p>
<p>5. <strong>Stroke</strong></p>
<p>6. <strong>Drug overdose/withdrawal</strong></p>
<p>7. <strong>Eclampsia</strong> (pregnancy + seizures)</p>
<p>8. <strong>Fever</strong> (febrile seizures in children 6 months-5 years)</p>
<h3>Prehospital Treatment</h3>
<h4>During Seizure (Active)</h4>
<table>
<tr><th>Do ✅</th><th>Don't ❌</th></tr>
<tr><td>Protect from injury (move objects away)</td><td><strong>Don't put anything in mouth</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>Position on side (recovery position)</td><td><strong>Don't hold down/restrain</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>O2 via NRB</td><td><strong>Don't try to stop the seizure</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>Suction if needed</td><td><strong>Don't give oral anything</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>Time the seizure</td><td><strong>Don't forget to check glucose</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>IV access (when seizure stops)</td><td></td></tr>
</table>
<h4>After Seizure (Post-Ictal)</h4>
<p>1. <strong>ABCs</strong> — Airway (suction if needed), Breathing, Circulation</p>
<p>2. <strong>Check glucose</strong> — Hypoglycemia is reversible cause!</p>
<p>3. <strong>O2</strong> — SpO2 monitoring</p>
<p>4. <strong>IV access</strong></p>
<p>5. <strong>Vital signs</strong> — Including temperature (fever?)</p>
<p>6. <strong>Assess for injuries</strong> — Tongue bite, head trauma, fractures</p>
<p>7. <strong>AMPLE history</strong> — Epilepsy? Meds? Compliance? Alcohol? Trauma?</p>
<p>8. <strong>Monitor</strong> — May seize again</p>
<h4>Pharmacological Treatment (ALS)</h4>
<table>
<tr><th>Medication</th><th>Dose</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Midazolam (Versed)</strong></td><td>5-10mg IM/IN/IV</td><td>First-line for status epilepticus</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Diazepam (Valium)</strong></td><td>5-10mg IV</td><td>Slower onset, longer duration</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Lorazepam (Ativan)</strong></td><td>2-4mg IV</td><td>Preferred in hospital (longer duration)</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Dextrose 50%</strong></td><td>25g IV</td><td>If hypoglycemic</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Thiamine</strong></td><td>100mg IV</td><td>Before glucose in alcoholics/malourished</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Magnesium sulfate</strong></td><td>4g IV</td><td>Eclampsia</td></tr>
</table>
<h3>Special Situations</h3>
<h4>Eclampsia</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Pregnancy ≥20 weeks</strong> + seizures + hypertension</li><li><strong>Treatment:</strong> Magnesium sulfate 4g IV over 10-20 min → maintenance 1-2g/hr</li><li><strong>Delivery</strong> is definitive treatment</li><li><strong>Risk:</strong> Placental abruption, DIC, fetal death</li></ul>
<h4>Febrile Seizures (เด็ก)</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Age:</strong> 6 months - 5 years</li><li><strong>Cause:</strong> Fever (usually &gt;38.5°C), not the height of fever but the rate of rise</li><li><strong>Simple:</strong> Generalized, &lt;15 minutes, no recurrence in 24 hours</li><li><strong>Complex:</strong> Focal, &gt;15 minutes, recurrence in 24 hours</li><li><strong>Treatment:</strong> Cooling measures, antipyretics, reassurance (benign condition)</li><li><strong>Important:</strong> Rule out meningitis in any febrile seizure!</li></ul>
<h4>Alcohol Withdrawal Seizures</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Timing:</strong> 6-48 hours after last drink</li><li><strong>Usually:</strong> Single generalized tonic-clonic, self-limiting</li><li><strong>Risk of progression to Delirium Tremens (DT):</strong> 24-72 hours</li><li><strong>DT signs:</strong> Agitation, tremor, hallucinations, tachycardia, hypertension, fever</li><li><strong>DT mortality:</strong> 5-15% if untreated</li><li><strong>Treatment:</strong> Benzodiazepines (diazepam, lorazepam)</li></ul>
<h4>Hypoglycemic Seizures</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Always check glucose</strong> in any seizure patient</li><li><strong>Treatment:</strong> D50 IV or Glucagon IM</li><li><strong>Seizure stops</strong> when glucose normalizes</li></ul>
<h4>Pseudoseizures (Psychogenic Non-Epileptic Seizures)</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Not true seizures</strong> — psychological origin</li><li><strong>Clues:</strong> Asynchronous movements, eye closure, no post-ictal confusion, no tongue bite, no incontinence, no cyanosis</li><li><strong>Diagnosis:</strong> EEG during episode (no epileptiform activity)</li><li><strong>Treatment:</strong> Reassurance, psychiatric referral</li></ul>
<h3>Post-Ictal State</h3>
<h4>Characteristics</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Confusion</strong> (most common)</li><li><strong>Fatigue/sleepiness</strong></li><li><strong>Headache</strong></li><li><strong>Muscle soreness</strong></li><li><strong>Amnesia</strong> for the event</li><li><strong>Duration:</strong> Minutes to hours</li></ul>
<h4>⚠️ Important</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Post-ictal confusion ≠ stroke</strong> — don't assume stroke in post-ictal patient</li><li><strong>If persistent focal deficit</strong> → think stroke (Todd's paralysis vs CVA)</li><li><strong>Todd's paralysis:</strong> Transient focal weakness after seizure (resolves in 24-48 hours)</li></ul>
<h3>Quiz</h3>
<p><strong>Q1:</strong> Status epilepticus คืออะไร?</p>
<p><strong>A1:</strong> <strong>Seizure ≥5 minutes</strong> OR <strong>≥2 seizures without regaining consciousness</strong> — medical emergency</p>
<p><strong>Q2:</strong> ขณะชัก ห้ามทำอะไร?</p>
<p><strong>A2:</strong> <strong>ห้ามใส่อะไรในปาก</strong> (tongue blade, fingers) — จะ cause injury; <strong>ห้าม hold down</strong> — จะ cause fractures</p>
<p><strong>Q3:</strong> Eclampsia treatment คืออะไร?</p>
<p><strong>A3:</strong> <strong>Magnesium sulfate 4g IV</strong> — first-line anticonvulsant for eclampsia</p>
<h3>ภาษาไทย: Seizures สำหรับ EMT</h3>
<ul><li><strong>Protect, don't restrain</strong> — ย้ายของ, ห้ามใส่อะไรในปาก</li><li><strong>Time the seizure</strong> — ≥5 min = status epilepticus</li><li><strong>Always check glucose</strong> — hypoglycemia is reversible!</li><li><strong>Post-ictal = normal</strong> — confusion after seizure is expected</li><li><strong>AMPLE history</strong> — epilepsy? meds? alcohol? trauma?</li></ul>
